course: everyday
lesson: 370
title: Standing by a friend
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Freddie — the boy who invented «Scaredy-Sam» — gets shoved into the big bins by two older boys. Sam doesn't fight anyone. He helps Freddie out, picks a banana skin off his jumper, walks him to the office and stays with him till his mum comes. Later Freddie is blamed for starting a fight he didn't start, and Sam goes with him to the head teacher to say what he saw. Freddie never says «Scaredy-Sam» again.
explain: One afternoon the Station Road boys corner Sam by the garages. Maddie walks over and simply stands next to him, arms folded, saying nothing. The boys look at her, look at each other, and drift off muttering. Nobody threw a punch. Sam's knees were knocking, but it was the most powerful silence he'd ever heard.
explain: Standing by someone takes many shapes. When Lily's usual friends fall out and leave her alone, Sam sits with her at lunch. Lily stays with Chloe in the nurse's room after Chloe's plaits are pulled so hard she cries. Kieran wants Maddie to help him «get back» at a Station Road boy; she says no — and stays his friend anyway. And when Jake's own friends start calling Sam names, Jake, surprisingly, tells them to stop.
explain: Jake's dad goes into hospital and Jake is angry at everything. Sam, of all people, lends him his best comic. Jake mumbles «ta». At the school disco a group laughs at Sam's dancing — which, to be fair, looks like a startled octopus. Lily walks over and dances right next to him, just as badly on purpose. Soon half the class is dancing like octopuses, and the laughing is a different kind.
fact: Standing by a friend means being with them and on their side, not fighting for them.
fact: Simply staying with someone who has been hurt, until an adult takes over, is a real help.
fact: Bullies often stop when the person they pick on is not alone.
fact: Telling the truth to a teacher about what you saw can save a friend from being wrongly blamed.
fact: A good friend can say no to a bad idea, like revenge, and still be a friend.
fact: Revenge usually starts a new round of trouble instead of ending it.
fact: People who are going through something hard at home, like a parent in hospital, often act angry; small kindnesses help.
fact: Telling your own friends to stop being unkind can be one of the hardest kinds of courage.
fact: Joining in alongside someone being laughed at can turn mocking into fun.
fact: Including someone who has been left alone at lunch or play is a simple, kind act.
exercise: When Freddie was shoved into the bins, how did Sam help? | He took him to the office and stayed with him | He went and fought the older boys by the bins | He laughed and walked away | He shoved the boys into the bins too
exercise: Why is it surprising that Sam helped Freddie? | Freddie was the one who called him «Scaredy-Sam» | Freddie was Sam's best friend from nursery onwards | Freddie was much bigger than Sam | Freddie had helped Sam before
exercise: How did Sam help when Freddie was wrongly blamed? | He told the head teacher what he really saw | He took the blame himself | He blamed the older boys without seeing what happened | He kept quiet to stay out of it
exercise: How did Maddie stop the Station Road boys without a fight? | She stood next to Sam so he wasn't alone | She punched the biggest boy first so the rest ran off | She shouted threats at them | She called the police on her phone
exercise: Why did the boys drift off when Maddie arrived? | Bullies often back off when the person isn't alone | They were scared that Maddie had a weapon with her | Their mums called them home | They wanted to be friends with Maddie
exercise: Why did Sam sit with Lily at lunch? | Her usual friends had left her alone | He wanted her pudding | The teacher made him sit there as a punishment | He wanted to annoy her friends
exercise: How did Lily stand by Chloe? | She stayed with her in the nurse's room | She went and pulled the boy's hair back | She told Chloe to stop crying and stop being a baby | She cut Chloe's plaits off
exercise: Kieran wanted revenge. What did Maddie do? | Said no but stayed his friend | Joined in to keep him happy | Dropped him as a friend forever | Told him to do it alone
exercise: Why is saying no to revenge a wise choice? | Revenge usually starts a new round of trouble | Revenge always ends the problem once and for all | The Station Road boy deserved nothing | Revenge is only wrong on weekdays
exercise: Why was it brave for Jake to tell his own friends to stop? | Going against your own friends is hard | His friends were very small | He wanted Sam's comic | Because Sam had paid him to tell his friends to stop
exercise: Why was Jake extra angry? | His dad was in hospital | Sam had taken his new comic | He lost a fight that morning | He just hates comics of every kind
exercise: Why did Sam's comic matter to Jake? | It was a small kindness when he was struggling | It was worth a lot of money if he sold it on | It told him how to win fights | It was a bribe to stop the names
exercise: At the disco, how did Lily change the laughing? | She danced next to Sam, turning mocking into fun | She told the whole group to get out of the hall | She laughed at Sam too | She turned off the music
exercise: Which of these is NOT standing by a friend? | Fighting the person who hurt them | Staying with them in the nurse's room | Telling the truth about what you saw | Sitting with them at lunch every day
exercise: Sam, Maddie, Lily and Jake all helped someone. What did none of them do? | Hit anyone | Stay with someone | Speak up for them | Show kindness
exercise: What does «Sam, of all people» show about him lending Jake the comic? | Jake had often been unkind to Sam, yet Sam was kind | Sam and Jake had always been best friends since nursery | Sam didn't like the comic | Sam had too many comics
test: «they shoved Freddie in the bins, let's go batter them». What's the better move? | Help Freddie and take him to a teacher | Go and batter them together as a gang | Laugh, he deserved it | Shove them in the bins too
test: Freddie's getting blamed for the fight and you saw it wasn't him. You hate grassing. What's right? | Tell the head what you saw | Say nothing and just walk away | Blame someone else at random | Tell Freddie to take the punishment
test: «why'd Maddie just stand there, she shoulda decked them». Who's right? | Maddie, standing by him stopped it without a fight | The speaker, decking them is what works best | Nobody, she should have run off | The Station Road boys, it was a joke
test: Lily's on her own at lunch, everyone's fallen out. you've got a seat. what do u do | Sit with her or invite her over | Ignore her, it's her drama | Tell everyone she's got no mates | Take a photo and post it
test: Kieran: «come on Mads, we'll get him at the garages after school». Best answer for Maddie? | «No, Kieran. I'm not doing that. Fancy a kickabout instead?» | «OK, I'll bring the others, we'll get him» | «Only if you go first» | «I'm never speaking to you again»
test: Jake's mates: «Scaredy-Sam! Scaredy-Sam!» Jake says «leave it out, lads». Why's that a big deal? | It's hard to stand up to your own friends | Jake just wanted them to be quiet | It means Jake is secretly scared of Sam | It isn't, it's nothing at all
test: «jake's been a nightmare all week, snapping at everyone». You find out his dad's in hospital. Best idea? | Be a bit kinder and maybe tell a teacher | Snap back at him twice as hard | Tell everyone in the class about his dad being in hospital | Ignore him for good
test: disco, everyone's laughing at a kid's dancing. which actually helps | Go and dance next to him | Laugh louder so he stops | Film it for the group chat | Tell him to sit down
test: «standing by a mate means fighting for him, obvs». Is it? | No, it means being with him and on his side | Yes, real friends always fight for each other | Yes, but only if he's smaller | No, it means leaving him alone
test: Chloe's crying in the nurse's room after her plaits got yanked. Lily's got lunch break. What's a kind choice? | Stay with her for a while | Go and pull the boy's hair back for her | Tell her plaits are silly anyway | Leave her, the nurse is there
test: If Maddie had joined Kieran's revenge, what would most likely have happened? | A new fight and more trouble for everyone | The Station Road boys would give up forever | Kieran would be happy and safe | The teachers would never find out
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the conduct of the pupil Maddie upon finding Sam surrounded by other boys? | Her silent presence beside him caused the boys to disperse without violence | She struck the boys and thereby resolved the matter | She summoned the police by means of a telephone carried for that purpose | She withdrew from the scene in order to avoid trouble
exam: Identify the principal reason why declining to participate in an act of revenge is prudent. | Revenge tends to precipitate further conflict | Revenge invariably concludes a dispute entirely | Revenge is permitted solely outside school premises | Declining necessarily terminates the friendship
exam: Given that a pupil is wrongly accused of having instigated a fight, ascertain the most appropriate conduct of a pupil who witnessed the incident. | To give a truthful account to the head teacher | To remain silent so as to avoid involvement | To accept responsibility on the accused pupil's behalf | To accuse a different pupil without evidence
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a pupil exhibiting anger whilst a parent is in hospital. | Small acts of kindness assist such a pupil | Such a pupil ought to be excluded by peers | Such anger indicates a wicked character | Such a pupil requires no consideration whatsoever
